      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "This isn't exactly a VRChat thing as much as it is a Unity thing and just a thing for 3D simulations in general. If you're too far away from the origin point of the game world (0,0,0), then the engine has to start estimating the closest position things are at. There's a couple of worlds showcasing this like this one: https://vrchat.com/home/world/wrld_b9f80349-74af-4840-8ce9-a1b783436590/info . It's called \"floating-point errors\" and like Docteh said, you basically have to change the way movement works in the game to make it not do stuff like that. (Basically you'd have to make the world itself move instead of making the player move, which I believe is what Unreal Engine does to get around this issue.) Floating point precision calculations are great for really fast calculations (and great for real-time rendering), but get more inaccurate as the numbers get larger. It's just kind of a limitation of computers and the math, I think.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
